 uracil and the  reason is thiamine cannot bond to the  sugar of RNA so that's why there's no  T's in RNA uracil can bind to the  different kind of sugar of RNA so that's  why you have used instead well let's  look at what RNA does well here's a  simple diagram of a cell and here's the  nucleus of the cell and inside the  nucleus is the DNA of the cell now as  we've said a few times  DNA is kind of like a set of  instructions of a blueprint and in DNA  are to be specific are the instructions  to build a protein but elsewhere in the  cell you have lots of parts of a cell  called a ribosome now ribosomes are  interesting because they're in the  cytoplasm but then you look at what they  do the ribosome is the actual builder of  the protein so you see we have a problem  we have the instructions to make the  protein in the nucleus but we have the  ribosome elsewhere in the cytoplasm so  something's missing  something must go in between the two and  that's the job of RNA RNA one of the  jobs of RNA is to make a temporary copy  of the DNA and carry it over to a  ribosome so there RNA is kind of like  the go between the intermediate molecule  it takes a copy of the DNA from the  nucleus over to the ribosome and now  that the ribosome has the instructions  to build a protein the ribosome will  begin to gather amino acids you might  know that amino acids are the monomers  or the building blocks of a protein and  so the ribosome gathers the amino acids  and they get linked  together to eventually make a protein  well let's do a little bit of review  before we're done so go ahead and pause  the video and try to fill in what goes  into box ABCDE NF pause the video some  of these were discussed other days of  course we only learned about nucleic  acids today but go ahead and try to fill  in as many of these as you can I'm gonna  go over the answer in three two one so a  monomer of a carbohydrate well that's a  monosaccharide a polymer of a  carbohydrate that's a polysaccharide  monomer of a protein that's an amino  acid a polymer of a protein that's a  polypeptide the monomer of a lipid  there's actually two of them a molecule  called fatty acid and a molecule called  glycerol and we talked about letter F  today the monomer of a nucleic acid  that's a nucleotide so how'd you do okay  so there you have it if you're in my  biology class you know pause the video  try to answer these questions on a  separate sheet of paper I'm happy to  check your answers before school or  after school one day so thanks for  watching hope you found this helpful
 

